content.text:
The first two app-server units (S10 U1–U2) are committed but not merged. Their full sign-off is queued to run after S8's, one heavy run at a time.

- **U1:** five new checks run against real run workers. They pin what a recorded run promises when it's closed and reopened:
  - a lost reply gets the same receipt back;
  - a stale close can't stop the new owner;
  - a changed source or seed blocks the restore and keeps the journal;
  - a crash mid-step restores only the finished steps;
  - accounts, reset, time zone and hidden credentials survive.

  All 5 pass.
- **U2:** two state machines, one for the app server and one for each run, in CIVIC's house style.
  - Their timeouts run on Effect's clock and are tested with a test clock, so no real waiting; 6 of 6 tests pass.
  - They aren't wired into the running code yet; that's the next unit (U3).
- **One failing test:** the worker's full browser run had one failure, the layout test "Actions stay fixed while versions scroll". It has failed under load before, then passed twice on its own and in four sign-offs, and S10 doesn't touch that file. The sign-off will confirm.
- **For U3:** the new libraries are build-time only for now. Outside apps install the canvas package from git and run its server code. So wiring the machines in will need Effect and XState as real dependencies, and must keep `@civic/core` out of anything that runs, because a git install can't resolve it. I wrote that into the commit message so it isn't lost.
